    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62161,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62162,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These lesions may be seen at any age and typically represent an incidental finding in a patient with Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome; non-syndromic cases may be associated with nonspecific symptoms and signs. Menstrual irregularities may be present. Rarely, non-syndromic cases may be associated with signs related to progesterone production {11493995; 5429475; 7104978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62163,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sex cord tumour with annular tubules is rare overall (accounting for &lt;&nbsp;1% of all sex cord tumours) but is commonly seen in patients with Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome {7104978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62164,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62165,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syndromic cases have germline <em>STK11</em> gene mutations on chromosome 19p13.3 {27821076}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62166,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range from small (microscopic to 3&nbsp;cm in size), bilateral, and multifocal lesions (usually syndromic) to sizable unilateral masses (non-syndromic). The latter are typically solid and tan to yellow, although cysts may be seen and occasionally may predominate. In syndromic cases, a gritty texture may be noted if there is a mass {5429475; 7104978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62167,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In both syndromic and non-syndromic cases, the lesions are characterized by variably sized, usually rounded nests composed of simple or complex tubules that encircle hyaline basement membrane&ndash;like material, which may also be present around the tubules. In Peutz&ndash;Jeghers lesions, extensive calcification within the tubules may be present. Cells are tall, with pale cytoplasm and basally located round nuclei that often display an antipodal distribution within the tubules. In syndrome-associated cases, a solid proliferation of indifferent sex cord cells or a Sertoli-like tubular pattern is occasionally seen. In non-syndromic cases, classic morphology may focally transition to granulosa or Sertoli cell morphology. Cytological atypia and mitotic activity may be seen, rarely, in the non-syndromic setting {5429475; 7104978}. The tumour cells are typically positive for calretinin, WT1, inhibin, SF1, FOXL2, and CD56 {12966351; 17581419; 21378549; 12808064}. They are typically negative for EMA and CD10 {17885484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62168,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
