        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62170,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> characteristic tubular pattern with antipodal distribution of nuclei and basement membrane-like material.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62171,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Malignant tumours are staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:19:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62172,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 80,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syndrome-associated tumours are typically benign, whereas non-syndromic cases exhibit extraovarian spread in as many as 20% of patients {5429475; 7104978}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:27:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]